6. UNIVERSAL DOWNLOAD EXPERIENCE — CRITICAL PRODUCT STANDARD

Establish one consistent download experience across all 68 tools.

Required four-state contract

State A — Ready to Process

Display one clear primary action:

- Run Merge PDF
- Convert to Word
- Generate Barcode
- Generate QR Code
- Protect PDF

No misleading Download button before a valid output exists.

State B — Processing

Display a separate readable processing area.

Examples:

- Preparing your document…
- Converting to Word…
- Compressing your PDF…
- Generating your Excel file…

Requirements:

- Never place long processing messages inside a truncated button.
- Never display raw internal JavaScript states.
- Never show fabricated percentage progress.
- Display progress percentage only when actual progress events support it.
- Keep the user informed without blocking the interface unnecessarily.

State C — Completed / Download Ready

Display exactly one canonical primary Download action for each output.

Use format-specific labels:

- Download PDF (.pdf)
- Download Word (.docx)
- Download Excel (.xlsx)
- Download PowerPoint (.pptx)
- Download JPG (.jpg)
- Download PNG (.png)
- Download ZIP (.zip)
- Download CSV (.csv)
- Download Text (.txt)

Requirements:

- Remove duplicate Download buttons.
- Remove ambiguous "Download File" labels when the output format is known.
- Never display confusing text such as "Downloading --", "Downloading --k", or truncated technical states.
- Never change the output filename to an incorrect extension.
- Keep Download available after a successful download.
- Allow safe repeated downloads.
- Keep "Process Another File" or "Reset" as a separate action.
- Clicking Download must never erase the generated result.
- Preserve the generated Blob until its download lifecycle is safely complete.

State D — Failure

Show a persistent, understandable error message.

Provide a safe Retry Download action when the output artifact remains valid.

If processing failed, explain the problem and provide an appropriate recovery action.

Never show raw stack traces or sensitive technical details to customers.

Download acceptance tests

Verify:

1. First download works.
2. Second download works.
3. Repeated download works.
4. Filename is correct.
5. MIME type is correct.
6. File opens in the appropriate application.
7. No reset occurs after download.
8. Slow mobile downloads do not lose their Blob URL prematurely.
9. Failed downloads have an understandable recovery path.
10. Only one canonical primary Download button is displayed.

Do not apply an arbitrary fixed delay as the only Blob lifecycle solution. Use a lifecycle-safe strategy supported by the browser and verify it through testing.

7. DOCUMENT FORMAT AND OUTPUT INTEGRITY

The output extension, MIME type, internal structure and actual document content must agree.

A successful conversion must produce a valid, usable file—not merely a Blob with the expected filename.

OpenXML validation

For DOCX, verify the ZIP structure and required internal files, including:

- [Content_Types].xml
- word/document.xml

For XLSX:

- [Content_Types].xml
- xl/workbook.xml

For PPTX:

- [Content_Types].xml
- ppt/presentation.xml

A ZIP PK signature alone is insufficient to certify a valid Office document.

Where supported, perform genuine file-opening tests in Microsoft Office or LibreOffice.

Image conversion

For PDF-to-JPG, PDF-to-PNG and PDF-to-Image:

- Single-page output should use a direct image download when promised by the tool.
- Multiple images may be delivered in ZIP.
- Validate JPEG magic bytes.
- Validate PNG magic bytes.
- Verify the actual extension and MIME type.
- Preserve image quality and page order.

Never silently deliver ZIP when the interface promises JPG or PNG.

Excel extension integrity

Do not offer .xls if the generated content is actually XLSX.

True XLS and XLSX formats must be distinguished correctly.

Display clear compatibility information whenever an export format has limitations.

PDF/A integrity

Do not claim ISO 19005 PDF/A compliance merely because a file has a .pdf extension.

Require genuine conformance verification before making a certification claim.

